# Every minute should automatically become a content package

Don't merely record the stream and manually cut things later.

The production runtime already knows:

```text
set start/end
camera cuts
captions
HAHA spikes
CLAP spikes
Ella score
Stream score
best laugh moment
character identity
```

So finishing a set should automatically generate:

| Asset                 | Purpose                |
| --------------------- | ---------------------- |
| `full-set-16x9.mp4`   | YouTube/Rumble/archive |
| `full-set-9x16.mp4`   | TikTok/Reels/Shorts    |
| `best-20s.mp4`        | viral hook             |
| `best-40s.mp4`        | longer social clip     |
| `score-reveal.mp4`    | panel content          |
| `ella-roast.mp4`      | reaction content       |
| thumbnail             | feed                   |
| captions `.srt/.json` | distribution           |
| performance JSON      | ML                     |

That is the actual distribution engine.

A single six-act episode might therefore produce **30–50 legitimate pieces of media** without creating AI sludge because they're all extracts from distinct performances.

And this matters for monetization. YouTube explicitly allows original AI-assisted/AI-generated creative work, and its AI disclosure by itself does not reduce monetization eligibility. What it is actively hostile to is generic, repetitive, mass-produced templated content. ([Google Help][1])

Freak Town has a natural defense against that: persistent characters, actual performances, live reactions, judging, evolving storylines and creator authorship. That is far more like an animated entertainment show than an AI-content farm.

