# The content flywheel gets ridiculous

Eventually:

```text
                    FREAK TOWN
                        │
            ┌───────────┼───────────┐
            ▼           ▼           ▼
        characters     clips      creators
            │           │           │
            ▼           ▼           ▼
       own socials    viral      reputation
            │           │           │
            └───────────┼───────────┘
                        ▼
                  NEW AUDIENCE
                        │
                        ▼
                   MAKE A FREAK
                        │
                        └────→ show
```

This is why I'd avoid over-monetizing it initially.

**The valuable scarce resource isn't the prize money. It's attention.**

A creator comes because:

> “My stupid character might perform in front of thousands of people.”

Then one person wakes up and discovers their ridiculous virtual pigeon has 400,000 followers.

That's the dream Freak Town should sell.

Money naturally attaches later through tips, appearances, ad revenue, sponsors, merch and character businesses.

And the timing is favorable for the content model: YouTube specifically says unique AI-created characters/narratives can qualify as original monetizable content, while AI disclosure itself doesn't suppress recommendation or monetization. ([Google Help][3])

So I would define Freak Town internally as:

> **An open talent show and launchpad for internet-native characters. Comedy is season one.**

That last distinction is important. The exact same infrastructure can eventually discover musicians, freakish talk-show hosts, rappers, storytellers, virtual wrestlers, improv characters and things we haven't named yet.

